{
  "manufacturer": "Woodward",
  "manufacturer_slug": "woodward",
  "model": "DSLC-2 Digital Synchronizer and Load Control",
  "model_slug": "dslc-2",
  "short_model": "DSLC-2",
  "aliases": [
    "DSLC2",
    "DSLC-2 synchronizer"
  ],
  "equipment_type": "Generator controller (synchronizer and load sharing control)",
  "applies_to": "DSLC-2, data protocol 5200, per manual 37443J.",
  "summary": "The DSLC-2 synchronises a generator to a bus and shares or controls its real and reactive load alongside the engine and voltage regulators. It is a Modbus RTU slave on RS-485 and a Modbus/TCP slave on Ethernet network B. Measurements and status come from data protocol 5200 at Modicon 450001; interface setpoints and remote discrete-input commands are written to parameters. This map covers frequencies, phase angle, 32-bit generator and bus values, energy, control modes, condition flags, alarms, relays and discrete inputs, and the interface setpoints.",
  "transport": [
    "RTU",
    "TCP"
  ],
  "defaults": {
    "unit_id": 1,
    "baud": 19200,
    "parity": "None",
    "data_bits": null,
    "stop_bits": 1,
    "tcp_port": null
  },
  "addressing": {
    "convention": "0-based",
    "note": "The 5200 table prints both the bus address (50000) and the Modicon address (450001); this map records and sends the bus address. Parameters use Modicon 40000 + (parameter ID + 1), so parameter 7642 is Modicon 47643 = wire address 7642; the manual's ModScan32 screenshots for 7642 (address 7643, 1-based) and 7640 (7641) confirm this. The worked examples print '407642' and '40509', which are typos that do not follow the manual's own rule."
  },
  "default_word_order": "ABCD",
  "firmware": null,
  "source": {
    "title": "DSLC-2 Digital Synchronizer and Load Control - Manual",
    "doc_number": "37443J",
    "revision_date": "2022-02-08",
    "url": "https://www.woodward.com/products/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2024/08/37443_J.pdf",
    "pages": "pp. 210-216 (Modbus address range, visualization, parameter rule, interface setpoints, discrete inputs over interface, loss of connection), p. 223 (Modbus parameters), pp. 230-239 (data protocol 5200), serial interface parameters in the configuration chapter"
  },
  "notes": [
    "The 5200 table states size (16/32 bit) and format (signed / bit array) for each value. The manual says 32-bit values are sent high word at the lower address (interface setpoint example), so 32-bit values are ABCD.",
    "Left out: the 16-bit power, reactive power, voltage and current values at 50011-50031, which are scaled by exponent registers 50001-50003; use the 32-bit values from 50100 instead. Also left out: values with only a range instead of a multiplier (remote load/process input AI4, process signal input AI5 and 50065/50066, power factor input and reference, ASA/CBS numbers) and unnamed bits.",
    "Load and reactive load control mode enums are shared with the MSLC-2 in the manual; only the DSLC-2 meanings are listed here.",
    "Interface setpoints need no password but only take effect when their setpoint-source parameter (7634, 7635, 7783, 7784) is set to Interface.",
    "The CB Aux and Modbus reset functions always stay hardware controlled even if released in 7645.",
    "Bits of 7644/7645 in manual order 01-16: Check, Permissive, Run, CB Aux, Voltage raise, Voltage lower, Base load, Load/Unload, Ramp pause, Load raise, Load lower, Process, Droop mode, Modbus reset, System update, N/A."
  ],
